  user_input {
    user_response: "I want you to build a beautiful, highly interactive educational website for my college subject \"Non-Chordata\".\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThis is an educational zoology website, not a generic portfolio or dashboard.\nThe website should feel like an immersive DIGITAL ZOOLOGY FIELD GUIDE / NATURAL HISTORY ATLAS.\n\nDo NOT make it look like a typical AI-generated website, admin dashboard, SaaS landing page, or overly futuristic website.\n\n==================================================\n1. PROJECT OBJECTIVE\n==================================================\n\nCreate an interactive website where students can explore the 5 major phyla of Non-Chordata and then explore important specimens belonging to each phylum.\n\nThe structure should be:\n\nNon-Chordata\n    \342\206\223\n5 Phyla\n    \342\206\223\nPhylum Details\n    \342\206\223\nSpecimens\n    \342\206\223\nIndividual Specimen Details\n    \342\206\223\nImage + Classification + Morphology + Habitat + Importance + Interesting Facts\n\nThe website should contain approximately 25 specimens, so there is NO need for a large database, backend, authentication system, or complex database infrastructure.\n\nThe content can initially be stored in well-structured local TypeScript/JSON data.\n\n==================================================\n2. PHYLA\n==================================================\n\nInclude exactly these 5 phyla:\n\n1. Porifera\n2. Cnidaria\n3. Ctenophora\n4. Platyhelminthes\n5. Nemathelminthes\n\nCreate a beautiful card/section for each phylum.\n\n==================================================\n3. SPECIMENS\n==================================================\n\nPORIFERA:\n- Sycon\n- Hyalonema\n- Euplectella\n- Spongilla\n\nCNIDARIA:\n- Obelia\n- Physalia\n- Millepora\n- Aurelia\n- Tubipora\n- Corallium\n- Alcyonium\n- Gorgonia\n- Adamsia\n- Pennatula\n- Fungia\n- Meandrina\n- Madrepora\n\nCTENOPHORA:\n- Beroe\n- Ctenoplana\n\nPLATYHELMINTHES:\n- Liver fluke (Fasciola)\n- Tapeworm (Taenia)\n\nNEMATHELMINTHES:\n- Ascaris\n\nIMPORTANT:\nSome names in the original list may have spelling variations. Use scientifically accepted/common zoological names while displaying the familiar specimen name where appropriate.\n\nFor example:\nObelisk \342\206\222 Obelia\nPhysician \342\206\222 Physalia\nMillipore \342\206\222 Millepora\nCoralline \342\206\222 Corallium\nGorgonian \342\206\222 Gorgonia\n\nWhere appropriate, show the scientific name in italics.\n\n==================================================\n4. VISUAL DESIGN\n==================================================\n\nThe most important design requirement:\n\nMake the website BEAUTIFUL, NATURAL, EARTHY and ORGANIC.\n\nThe visual identity should be inspired by:\n- Forests\n- Natural history museums\n- Botanical field guides\n- Old zoology textbooks\n- Earth\n- Leaves\n- Moss\n- Wood\n- Parchment\n- Natural textures\n- Scientific specimen collections\n\nDO NOT use:\n- Dark blue/black futuristic AI aesthetics\n- Neon gradients\n- Excessive glassmorphism\n- Generic SaaS cards\n- Corporate dashboard styling\n- Excessive animations\n- Cyberpunk aesthetics\n\nUse an earthy palette such as:\n- Forest green\n- Moss green\n- Sage\n- Olive\n- Warm beige\n- Cream\n- Sand\n- Terracotta\n- Brown\n- Off-white\n\nThe background should feel alive and natural.\n\nUse subtle:\n- botanical silhouettes\n- leaf patterns\n- paper/grain texture\n- organic curves\n- subtle watercolor-like shapes\n- very light scientific illustrations\n\nThese should remain subtle and must not reduce readability.\n\n==================================================\n5. HERO SECTION\n==================================================\n\nCreate a stunning landing page hero.\n\nMain title:\n\n\"NON-CHORDATA\"\n\nSubtitle:\n\n\"An Interactive Journey Through the Hidden World of Invertebrate Life\"\n\nSupporting text:\n\n\"Explore the diversity, structure, classification and fascinating biology of five major non-chordate phyla through specimens, illustrations and interactive learning.\"\n\nAdd a prominent CTA:\n\n\"Explore the Phyla\"\n\nWhen clicked, smoothly scroll to the phyla section.\n\nThe hero background should have a beautiful natural/underwater-inspired visual composition.\n\nPossible visual elements:\n- subtle leaves\n- marine organisms\n- coral silhouettes\n- sponge forms\n- microscopic-inspired patterns\n- botanical/natural-history illustrations\n\nKeep it elegant rather than cluttered.\n\n==================================================\n6. NAVIGATION\n==================================================\n\nCreate a clean sticky navigation bar.\n\nNavigation:\n\nHome\nPhyla\nSpecimens\nExplore\nAbout\n\nAdd a small search icon/input.\n\nThe navbar should become slightly more solid when scrolling.\n\nOn mobile, convert it into a hamburger menu.\n\n==================================================\n7. PHYLA EXPLORATION SECTION\n==================================================\n\nCreate a visually impressive section:\n\n\"Explore the Five Phyla\"\n\nDisplay 5 large interactive cards:\n\nPorifera\nCnidaria\nCtenophora\nPlatyhelminthes\nNemathelminthes\n\nEach card should contain:\n- Phylum name\n- Short description\n- Number of specimens\n- Representative visual\n- \"Explore Phylum\" button\n\nAdd subtle hover interactions:\n- card slightly lifts\n- image gently zooms\n- organic background animation\n- button appears/highlights\n\nDo not overdo animations.\n\n==================================================\n8. PHYLLUM DETAIL PAGE / VIEW\n==================================================\n\nWhen the user clicks a phylum, open a dedicated detailed view/page.\n\nExample:\n\nPORIFERA\n\nInclude:\n\n1. Overview\n2. General Characteristics\n3. Habitat\n4. Body Organization\n5. Symmetry\n6. Level of Organization\n7. Body Wall\n8. Canal System\n9. Digestion\n10. Respiration\n11. Excretion\n12. Reproduction\n13. Skeleton\n14. Economic/Ecological Importance\n15. Important Examples\n\nThen display all specimens belonging to that phylum.\n\nAdd a visual \"At a Glance\" section with information such as:\n\nKingdom: Animalia\nPhylum: Porifera\nOrganization: Cellular\nSymmetry: Usually asymmetrical\nHabitat: Mostly aquatic\nExamples: Sycon, Hyalonema, Euplectella, Spongilla\n\nUse scientifically accurate educational content suitable for undergraduate zoology students.\n\n==================================================\n9. SPECIMEN CARDS\n==================================================\n\nWithin each phylum page, display specimen cards.\n\nEach card should include:\n\n- Specimen image\n- Common name\n- Scientific name\n- 1-line identification\n- Habitat\n- \"View Specimen\" button\n\nExample:\n\nSYCON\nScientific name: Sycon\n\n\"View Specimen \342\206\222\"\n\nCards should feel like natural history museum specimen labels.\n\n==================================================\n10. INDIVIDUAL SPECIMEN EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nWhen a student clicks a specimen, open a beautiful dedicated specimen detail page or large interactive modal.\n\nExample:\n\nSYCON\n\nDisplay a large high-quality image.\n\nThen show:\n\nScientific Classification\n-------------------------\nKingdom:\nPhylum:\nClass:\nOrder:\nGenus:\nSpecies:\n\nThen:\n\nIdentification\n\nHabitat\n\nExternal Morphology\n\nInternal Structure\n\nCanal System / Special Features\n\nMode of Nutrition\n\nReproduction\n\nLife Cycle (where relevant)\n\nEcological Importance\n\nEconomic/Medical Importance (where relevant)\n\nInteresting Facts\n\nExam Points\n\nUse clear sections and visual hierarchy.\n\n==================================================\n11. SPECIMEN INFORMATION\n==================================================\n\nFor EVERY specimen, prepare structured educational data.\n\nThe content should be concise enough to read but detailed enough for college examination preparation.\n\nEach specimen should have:\n\n{\n  id,\n  commonName,\n  scientificName,\n  phylum,\n  classification,\n  image,\n  shortDescription,\n  habitat,\n  morphology,\n  anatomy,\n  nutrition,\n  reproduction,\n  specialFeatures,\n  importance,\n  interestingFacts,\n  examPoints\n}\n\nDo NOT make up obviously false scientific information.\n\nWhere species-level information varies, use the representative/common textbook description.\n\n==================================================\n12. INTERACTIVE \"EXPLORE\" EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nAdd an interactive section called:\n\n\"Explore the Living World\"\n\nPossible interactions:\n\nA. PHYLUM FILTER\nAllow users to filter specimens by phylum.\n\nB. SEARCH\nSearch:\n- specimen name\n- scientific name\n- phylum\n\nExample:\nSearch \"Sycon\"\n\342\206\222 Sycon specimen appears.\n\nSearch \"Cnidaria\"\n\342\206\222 Cnidarian specimens appear.\n\nC. QUICK FACTS\nDisplay random interesting zoology facts.\n\nExample:\n\"Did you know?\nEuplectella is commonly known as the Venus flower basket.\"\n\nD. SPECIMEN OF THE DAY\nHighlight one specimen randomly or using a predefined rotation.\n\nE. COMPARE\nAllow the user to select two specimens and compare:\n\n- Phylum\n- Habitat\n- Symmetry\n- Organization\n- Body structure\n- Nutrition\n- Reproduction\n- Special features\n\nMake the comparison visual.\n\n==================================================\n13. INTERACTIVE CLASSIFICATION TREE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a beautiful interactive classification tree:\n\nAnimalia\n   |\n   +-- Porifera\n   |     +-- Sycon\n   |     +-- Hyalonema\n   |     +-- Euplectella\n   |     +-- Spongilla\n   |\n   +-- Cnidaria\n   |     +-- Obelia\n   |     +-- Physalia\n   |     +-- ...\n   |\n   +-- Ctenophora\n   |\n   +-- Platyhelminthes\n   |\n   +-- Nemathelminthes\n\nClicking a node should open the corresponding information.\n\nMake this visually elegant and interactive.\n\n==================================================\n14. \"FIELD GUIDE\" MODE\n==================================================\n\nAdd a special feature called:\n\n\"Field Guide\"\n\nThe user enters Field Guide mode and sees specimen cards arranged like pages of an old naturalist\'s field journal.\n\nEach specimen should have:\n- specimen illustration/photo\n- scientific name\n- short identification\n- habitat\n- classification\n\nAllow navigation:\n\n\342\206\220 Previous specimen\n\342\206\222 Next specimen\n\nThis should feel like flipping through a zoological field guide.\n\n==================================================\n15. QUIZ MODE\n==================================================\n\nAdd an interactive quiz section.\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Test Your Zoology\"\n\nGenerate questions from the specimen data.\n\nQuestion types:\n\n- MCQs\n- Identify the specimen\n- Identify the phylum\n- Match specimen with phylum\n- True/False\n\nExample:\n\n\"Which phylum does Sycon belong to?\"\n\nA. Cnidaria\nB. Porifera\nC. Ctenophora\nD. Platyhelminthes\n\nAfter answering:\n- show correct/incorrect\n- explain the answer\n- update score\n\nAt the end:\n\n\"Your Score: 8/10\"\n\nInclude:\n\"Try Again\"\n\n==================================================\n16. EXAM MODE\n==================================================\n\nAdd another feature:\n\n\"Exam Revision\"\n\nShow compact revision cards.\n\nExample:\n\nPORIFERA \342\200\224 QUICK REVISION\n\n\342\200\242 Cellular level of organization\n\342\200\242 Mostly aquatic\n\342\200\242 Canal system present\n\342\200\242 Choanocytes present\n\342\200\242 Skeleton made of spicules/spongin\n\342\200\242 Mostly asymmetrical\n\342\200\242 Reproduction may be sexual or asexual\n\nFor each phylum create a concise revision sheet.\n\nAlso create:\n\"Important Specimens to Remember\"\n\n==================================================\n17. SPECIMEN IMAGE SYSTEM\n==================================================\n\nImages are extremely important.\n\nUse high-quality scientifically accurate specimen photographs/illustrations wherever possible.\n\nFor each specimen:\n- large hero image\n- thumbnail\n- optional secondary image\n- image caption\n- scientific name\n\nDO NOT use unrelated decorative images.\n\nIf external image URLs are used, structure the data so images can easily be replaced later.\n\nCreate a central image mapping/data file so I can easily replace image URLs.\n\nFor example:\n\nconst specimenImages = {\n  sycon: \"...\",\n  hyalonema: \"...\",\n  euplectella: \"...\",\n}\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not hard-code image URLs throughout the components.\n\n==================================================\n18. TECHNOLOGY\n==================================================\n\nUse:\n\nReact\nTypeScript\nVite\n\nUse a clean component architecture.\n\nRecommended:\n\nsrc/\n  components/\n  pages/\n  data/\n  types/\n  assets/\n  utils/\n\nSuggested components:\n\nNavbar\nHero\nPhylumCard\nPhylumGrid\nPhylumDetail\nSpecimenCard\nSpecimenGrid\nSpecimenDetail\nClassificationTree\nSearchBar\nComparePanel\nQuiz\nRevisionMode\nFieldGuide\nFooter\n\nUse React Router for navigation.\n\nUse local structured data rather than a backend database.\n\n==================================================\n19. RESPONSIVE DESIGN\n==================================================\n\nThe website must work beautifully on:\n\nDesktop\nLaptop\nTablet\nMobile\n\nOn mobile:\n- cards should stack properly\n- navigation should collapse\n- images should remain readable\n- specimen details should be easy to scroll\n- quiz should work comfortably with touch\n\n==================================================\n20. ANIMATIONS\n==================================================\n\nUse tasteful animations.\n\nExamples:\n- fade-in on scroll\n- smooth section transitions\n- card hover\n- image zoom\n- page transitions\n- subtle floating botanical elements\n- smooth scrolling\n- specimen modal animation\n\nAnimations must be subtle and elegant.\n\nAvoid:\n- excessive bouncing\n- flashy transitions\n- distracting animations\n\nThe website should feel calm and premium.\n\n==================================================\n21. TYPOGRAPHY\n==================================================\n\nUse a combination of:\n\nElegant serif font for major headings.\n\nClean sans-serif font for body text.\n\nThe overall typography should resemble:\n- natural history books\n- scientific journals\n- museum exhibits\n\nMake scientific names italic.\n\n==================================================\n22. BACKGROUND\n==================================================\n\nCreate a layered background system.\n\nBase:\nwarm cream / parchment tone.\n\nSecondary:\nvery subtle paper grain.\n\nDecorative:\nfaint leaves / botanical forms / marine silhouettes.\n\nSome sections can transition into:\nsage green\nforest green\nwarm beige\nsoft terracotta\n\nThe background should visually connect the entire website.\n\n==================================================\n23. FOOTER\n==================================================\n\nCreate a simple elegant footer.\n\nInclude:\n\n\"NON-CHORDATA\"\n\n\"An Interactive Zoology Field Guide\"\n\nSections:\nExplore\nPhyla\nSpecimens\nRevision\nQuiz\n\nAdd:\n\n\"Built as an educational resource for studying Non-Chordata.\"\n\n==================================================\n24. IMPORTANT UX REQUIREMENTS\n==================================================\n\nThe website should never feel overwhelming.\n\nUse progressive disclosure.\n\nShow:\noverview first\n\342\206\222 details when requested\n\342\206\222 deeper information inside specimen pages\n\nAdd breadcrumbs:\n\nHome\n\342\206\222 Phyla\n\342\206\222 Porifera\n\342\206\222 Sycon\n\nAdd a \"Back to Phylum\" button.\n\nAdd \"Previous Specimen\" and \"Next Specimen\" navigation.\n\n==================================================\n25. DATA ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nSince there are only around 25 specimens, DO NOT create a complicated backend.\n\nCreate a structured data file:\n\nsrc/data/nonChordata.ts\n\nExample:\n\nexport const phyla = [...]\n\nexport const specimens = [...]\n\nEach specimen should reference its phylum using an ID.\n\nExample:\n\n{\n  id: \"sycon\",\n  name: \"Sycon\",\n  scientificName: \"Sycon\",\n  phylumId: \"porifera\",\n  description: \"...\",\n  habitat: \"...\",\n  morphology: \"...\",\n  reproduction: \"...\",\n  importance: \"...\",\n  examPoints: [...]\n}\n\nThis should make it extremely easy to add new specimens later.\n\n==================================================\n26. SEARCH ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nImplement client-side search.\n\nSearch should instantly search through:\n\n- Phylum names\n- Specimen names\n- Scientific names\n- Keywords\n\nShow results in a clean dropdown.\n\nExample:\n\nSearch: \"sponge\"\n\nResults:\nPorifera\nSycon\nHyalonema\nEuplectella\nSpongilla\n\n==================================================\n27. PERFORMANCE\n==================================================\n\nKeep the website lightweight.\n\nDo not add unnecessary libraries.\n\nOptimize images.\n\nLazy-load specimen images where appropriate.\n\nAvoid unnecessary API calls.\n\nNo backend is required for the initial version.\n\n==================================================\n28. ACCESSIBILITY\n==================================================\n\nUse:\n- semantic HTML\n- alt text for images\n- keyboard navigation\n- accessible buttons\n- readable contrast\n- proper heading hierarchy\n\n==================================================\n29. CONTENT QUALITY\n==================================================\n\nThe content is intended for undergraduate zoology study.\n\nMake information:\n- scientifically accurate\n- structured\n- exam-friendly\n- understandable\n- concise but sufficiently detailed\n\nAvoid overly complicated research-level terminology unless it is explained.\n\nFor important terminology, add small tooltips.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Choanocytes\"\n\342\206\222 \"Specialized flagellated cells involved in water current generation and food capture in sponges.\"\n\n==================================================\n30. SPECIAL VISUAL FEATURE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a \"Specimen Spotlight\" component.\n\nWhen the user opens a specimen, show:\n\n----------------------------------\nSPECIMEN SPOTLIGHT\n\n        [IMAGE]\n\n        SYCON\n        Porifera\n\n        \"A simple but fascinating\n         example of a sponge.\"\n\n        Habitat: Marine\n        Organization: Cellular\n\n        [Explore Anatomy]\n----------------------------------\n\nUse a subtle natural-paper card design.\n\n==================================================\n31. ANATOMY INTERACTION\n==================================================\n\nWhere appropriate, create simple interactive anatomy diagrams.\n\nFor example, for Sycon:\n\nOsculum\n\342\206\223\nSpongocoel\n\342\206\223\nRadial canals\n\342\206\223\nIncurrent canals\n\342\206\223\nOstia\n\nClicking a label should show a short explanation.\n\nFor other organisms, only create anatomy diagrams where they are genuinely useful.\n\nDo not fabricate scientifically inaccurate diagrams.\n\n==================================================\n32. FINAL VISUAL DIRECTION\n==================================================\n\nImagine this website as a combination of:\n\nNatural History Museum\n+\nUniversity Zoology Lab\n+\nVintage Field Journal\n+\nModern Interactive Educational Website\n\nIt should feel:\n\nElegant\nNatural\nScientific\nEducational\nInteractive\nCalm\nPremium\nImmersive\n\nNOT:\n\nCorporate\nCyberpunk\nAI-generated-looking\nOverly futuristic\nGeneric\nDashboard-like\n\n==================================================\n33. DEVELOPMENT REQUIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nBuild the website completely.\n\nDo not just create a landing page.\n\nAll major interactions must actually work:\n\n\342\234\223 Phylum navigation\n\342\234\223 Specimen navigation\n\342\234\223 Search\n\342\234\223 Filters\n\342\234\223 Specimen details\n\342\234\223 Classification tree\n\342\234\223 Field Guide mode\n\342\234\223 Quiz\n\342\234\223 Revision mode\n\342\234\223 Compare specimens\n\342\234\223 Responsive navigation\n\342\234\223 Previous/Next specimen\n\342\234\223 Breadcrumb navigation\n\nUse realistic sample content for all listed specimens.\n\nDo not leave empty placeholder sections such as:\n\"Lorem ipsum\"\n\"Coming soon\"\n\"Add content here\"\n\n==================================================\n34. FINAL CHECK\n==================================================\n\nBefore finishing, verify:\n\n1. All 5 phyla exist.\n2. All listed specimens exist.\n3. Every specimen opens its own detail view.\n4. Every specimen has an image field.\n5. Search works.\n6. Filters work.\n7. Quiz works.\n8. Comparison works.\n9. Field Guide works.\n10. Revision mode works.\n11. Navigation works.\n12. Mobile layout works.\n13. No broken routes.\n14. No console errors.\n15. No placeholder content.\n16. The visual design consistently follows the earthy/natural-history theme.\n\nStart by creating the complete frontend architecture and data model, then implement the visual design and interactions.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not over-engineer this project.\nThere are only 5 phyla and approximately 25 specimens.\nPrioritize visual quality, educational usefulness, interaction and a polished user experience over backend complexity."
    items {
      text: "I want you to build a beautiful, highly interactive educational website for my college subject \"Non-Chordata\".\n\nIMPORTANT:\nThis is an educational zoology website, not a generic portfolio or dashboard.\nThe website should feel like an immersive DIGITAL ZOOLOGY FIELD GUIDE / NATURAL HISTORY ATLAS.\n\nDo NOT make it look like a typical AI-generated website, admin dashboard, SaaS landing page, or overly futuristic website.\n\n==================================================\n1. PROJECT OBJECTIVE\n==================================================\n\nCreate an interactive website where students can explore the 5 major phyla of Non-Chordata and then explore important specimens belonging to each phylum.\n\nThe structure should be:\n\nNon-Chordata\n    \342\206\223\n5 Phyla\n    \342\206\223\nPhylum Details\n    \342\206\223\nSpecimens\n    \342\206\223\nIndividual Specimen Details\n    \342\206\223\nImage + Classification + Morphology + Habitat + Importance + Interesting Facts\n\nThe website should contain approximately 25 specimens, so there is NO need for a large database, backend, authentication system, or complex database infrastructure.\n\nThe content can initially be stored in well-structured local TypeScript/JSON data.\n\n==================================================\n2. PHYLA\n==================================================\n\nInclude exactly these 5 phyla:\n\n1. Porifera\n2. Cnidaria\n3. Ctenophora\n4. Platyhelminthes\n5. Nemathelminthes\n\nCreate a beautiful card/section for each phylum.\n\n==================================================\n3. SPECIMENS\n==================================================\n\nPORIFERA:\n- Sycon\n- Hyalonema\n- Euplectella\n- Spongilla\n\nCNIDARIA:\n- Obelia\n- Physalia\n- Millepora\n- Aurelia\n- Tubipora\n- Corallium\n- Alcyonium\n- Gorgonia\n- Adamsia\n- Pennatula\n- Fungia\n- Meandrina\n- Madrepora\n\nCTENOPHORA:\n- Beroe\n- Ctenoplana\n\nPLATYHELMINTHES:\n- Liver fluke (Fasciola)\n- Tapeworm (Taenia)\n\nNEMATHELMINTHES:\n- Ascaris\n\nIMPORTANT:\nSome names in the original list may have spelling variations. Use scientifically accepted/common zoological names while displaying the familiar specimen name where appropriate.\n\nFor example:\nObelisk \342\206\222 Obelia\nPhysician \342\206\222 Physalia\nMillipore \342\206\222 Millepora\nCoralline \342\206\222 Corallium\nGorgonian \342\206\222 Gorgonia\n\nWhere appropriate, show the scientific name in italics.\n\n==================================================\n4. VISUAL DESIGN\n==================================================\n\nThe most important design requirement:\n\nMake the website BEAUTIFUL, NATURAL, EARTHY and ORGANIC.\n\nThe visual identity should be inspired by:\n- Forests\n- Natural history museums\n- Botanical field guides\n- Old zoology textbooks\n- Earth\n- Leaves\n- Moss\n- Wood\n- Parchment\n- Natural textures\n- Scientific specimen collections\n\nDO NOT use:\n- Dark blue/black futuristic AI aesthetics\n- Neon gradients\n- Excessive glassmorphism\n- Generic SaaS cards\n- Corporate dashboard styling\n- Excessive animations\n- Cyberpunk aesthetics\n\nUse an earthy palette such as:\n- Forest green\n- Moss green\n- Sage\n- Olive\n- Warm beige\n- Cream\n- Sand\n- Terracotta\n- Brown\n- Off-white\n\nThe background should feel alive and natural.\n\nUse subtle:\n- botanical silhouettes\n- leaf patterns\n- paper/grain texture\n- organic curves\n- subtle watercolor-like shapes\n- very light scientific illustrations\n\nThese should remain subtle and must not reduce readability.\n\n==================================================\n5. HERO SECTION\n==================================================\n\nCreate a stunning landing page hero.\n\nMain title:\n\n\"NON-CHORDATA\"\n\nSubtitle:\n\n\"An Interactive Journey Through the Hidden World of Invertebrate Life\"\n\nSupporting text:\n\n\"Explore the diversity, structure, classification and fascinating biology of five major non-chordate phyla through specimens, illustrations and interactive learning.\"\n\nAdd a prominent CTA:\n\n\"Explore the Phyla\"\n\nWhen clicked, smoothly scroll to the phyla section.\n\nThe hero background should have a beautiful natural/underwater-inspired visual composition.\n\nPossible visual elements:\n- subtle leaves\n- marine organisms\n- coral silhouettes\n- sponge forms\n- microscopic-inspired patterns\n- botanical/natural-history illustrations\n\nKeep it elegant rather than cluttered.\n\n==================================================\n6. NAVIGATION\n==================================================\n\nCreate a clean sticky navigation bar.\n\nNavigation:\n\nHome\nPhyla\nSpecimens\nExplore\nAbout\n\nAdd a small search icon/input.\n\nThe navbar should become slightly more solid when scrolling.\n\nOn mobile, convert it into a hamburger menu.\n\n==================================================\n7. PHYLA EXPLORATION SECTION\n==================================================\n\nCreate a visually impressive section:\n\n\"Explore the Five Phyla\"\n\nDisplay 5 large interactive cards:\n\nPorifera\nCnidaria\nCtenophora\nPlatyhelminthes\nNemathelminthes\n\nEach card should contain:\n- Phylum name\n- Short description\n- Number of specimens\n- Representative visual\n- \"Explore Phylum\" button\n\nAdd subtle hover interactions:\n- card slightly lifts\n- image gently zooms\n- organic background animation\n- button appears/highlights\n\nDo not overdo animations.\n\n==================================================\n8. PHYLLUM DETAIL PAGE / VIEW\n==================================================\n\nWhen the user clicks a phylum, open a dedicated detailed view/page.\n\nExample:\n\nPORIFERA\n\nInclude:\n\n1. Overview\n2. General Characteristics\n3. Habitat\n4. Body Organization\n5. Symmetry\n6. Level of Organization\n7. Body Wall\n8. Canal System\n9. Digestion\n10. Respiration\n11. Excretion\n12. Reproduction\n13. Skeleton\n14. Economic/Ecological Importance\n15. Important Examples\n\nThen display all specimens belonging to that phylum.\n\nAdd a visual \"At a Glance\" section with information such as:\n\nKingdom: Animalia\nPhylum: Porifera\nOrganization: Cellular\nSymmetry: Usually asymmetrical\nHabitat: Mostly aquatic\nExamples: Sycon, Hyalonema, Euplectella, Spongilla\n\nUse scientifically accurate educational content suitable for undergraduate zoology students.\n\n==================================================\n9. SPECIMEN CARDS\n==================================================\n\nWithin each phylum page, display specimen cards.\n\nEach card should include:\n\n- Specimen image\n- Common name\n- Scientific name\n- 1-line identification\n- Habitat\n- \"View Specimen\" button\n\nExample:\n\nSYCON\nScientific name: Sycon\n\n\"View Specimen \342\206\222\"\n\nCards should feel like natural history museum specimen labels.\n\n==================================================\n10. INDIVIDUAL SPECIMEN EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nWhen a student clicks a specimen, open a beautiful dedicated specimen detail page or large interactive modal.\n\nExample:\n\nSYCON\n\nDisplay a large high-quality image.\n\nThen show:\n\nScientific Classification\n-------------------------\nKingdom:\nPhylum:\nClass:\nOrder:\nGenus:\nSpecies:\n\nThen:\n\nIdentification\n\nHabitat\n\nExternal Morphology\n\nInternal Structure\n\nCanal System / Special Features\n\nMode of Nutrition\n\nReproduction\n\nLife Cycle (where relevant)\n\nEcological Importance\n\nEconomic/Medical Importance (where relevant)\n\nInteresting Facts\n\nExam Points\n\nUse clear sections and visual hierarchy.\n\n==================================================\n11. SPECIMEN INFORMATION\n==================================================\n\nFor EVERY specimen, prepare structured educational data.\n\nThe content should be concise enough to read but detailed enough for college examination preparation.\n\nEach specimen should have:\n\n{\n  id,\n  commonName,\n  scientificName,\n  phylum,\n  classification,\n  image,\n  shortDescription,\n  habitat,\n  morphology,\n  anatomy,\n  nutrition,\n  reproduction,\n  specialFeatures,\n  importance,\n  interestingFacts,\n  examPoints\n}\n\nDo NOT make up obviously false scientific information.\n\nWhere species-level information varies, use the representative/common textbook description.\n\n==================================================\n12. INTERACTIVE \"EXPLORE\" EXPERIENCE\n==================================================\n\nAdd an interactive section called:\n\n\"Explore the Living World\"\n\nPossible interactions:\n\nA. PHYLUM FILTER\nAllow users to filter specimens by phylum.\n\nB. SEARCH\nSearch:\n- specimen name\n- scientific name\n- phylum\n\nExample:\nSearch \"Sycon\"\n\342\206\222 Sycon specimen appears.\n\nSearch \"Cnidaria\"\n\342\206\222 Cnidarian specimens appear.\n\nC. QUICK FACTS\nDisplay random interesting zoology facts.\n\nExample:\n\"Did you know?\nEuplectella is commonly known as the Venus flower basket.\"\n\nD. SPECIMEN OF THE DAY\nHighlight one specimen randomly or using a predefined rotation.\n\nE. COMPARE\nAllow the user to select two specimens and compare:\n\n- Phylum\n- Habitat\n- Symmetry\n- Organization\n- Body structure\n- Nutrition\n- Reproduction\n- Special features\n\nMake the comparison visual.\n\n==================================================\n13. INTERACTIVE CLASSIFICATION TREE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a beautiful interactive classification tree:\n\nAnimalia\n   |\n   +-- Porifera\n   |     +-- Sycon\n   |     +-- Hyalonema\n   |     +-- Euplectella\n   |     +-- Spongilla\n   |\n   +-- Cnidaria\n   |     +-- Obelia\n   |     +-- Physalia\n   |     +-- ...\n   |\n   +-- Ctenophora\n   |\n   +-- Platyhelminthes\n   |\n   +-- Nemathelminthes\n\nClicking a node should open the corresponding information.\n\nMake this visually elegant and interactive.\n\n==================================================\n14. \"FIELD GUIDE\" MODE\n==================================================\n\nAdd a special feature called:\n\n\"Field Guide\"\n\nThe user enters Field Guide mode and sees specimen cards arranged like pages of an old naturalist\'s field journal.\n\nEach specimen should have:\n- specimen illustration/photo\n- scientific name\n- short identification\n- habitat\n- classification\n\nAllow navigation:\n\n\342\206\220 Previous specimen\n\342\206\222 Next specimen\n\nThis should feel like flipping through a zoological field guide.\n\n==================================================\n15. QUIZ MODE\n==================================================\n\nAdd an interactive quiz section.\n\nTitle:\n\n\"Test Your Zoology\"\n\nGenerate questions from the specimen data.\n\nQuestion types:\n\n- MCQs\n- Identify the specimen\n- Identify the phylum\n- Match specimen with phylum\n- True/False\n\nExample:\n\n\"Which phylum does Sycon belong to?\"\n\nA. Cnidaria\nB. Porifera\nC. Ctenophora\nD. Platyhelminthes\n\nAfter answering:\n- show correct/incorrect\n- explain the answer\n- update score\n\nAt the end:\n\n\"Your Score: 8/10\"\n\nInclude:\n\"Try Again\"\n\n==================================================\n16. EXAM MODE\n==================================================\n\nAdd another feature:\n\n\"Exam Revision\"\n\nShow compact revision cards.\n\nExample:\n\nPORIFERA \342\200\224 QUICK REVISION\n\n\342\200\242 Cellular level of organization\n\342\200\242 Mostly aquatic\n\342\200\242 Canal system present\n\342\200\242 Choanocytes present\n\342\200\242 Skeleton made of spicules/spongin\n\342\200\242 Mostly asymmetrical\n\342\200\242 Reproduction may be sexual or asexual\n\nFor each phylum create a concise revision sheet.\n\nAlso create:\n\"Important Specimens to Remember\"\n\n==================================================\n17. SPECIMEN IMAGE SYSTEM\n==================================================\n\nImages are extremely important.\n\nUse high-quality scientifically accurate specimen photographs/illustrations wherever possible.\n\nFor each specimen:\n- large hero image\n- thumbnail\n- optional secondary image\n- image caption\n- scientific name\n\nDO NOT use unrelated decorative images.\n\nIf external image URLs are used, structure the data so images can easily be replaced later.\n\nCreate a central image mapping/data file so I can easily replace image URLs.\n\nFor example:\n\nconst specimenImages = {\n  sycon: \"...\",\n  hyalonema: \"...\",\n  euplectella: \"...\",\n}\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not hard-code image URLs throughout the components.\n\n==================================================\n18. TECHNOLOGY\n==================================================\n\nUse:\n\nReact\nTypeScript\nVite\n\nUse a clean component architecture.\n\nRecommended:\n\nsrc/\n  components/\n  pages/\n  data/\n  types/\n  assets/\n  utils/\n\nSuggested components:\n\nNavbar\nHero\nPhylumCard\nPhylumGrid\nPhylumDetail\nSpecimenCard\nSpecimenGrid\nSpecimenDetail\nClassificationTree\nSearchBar\nComparePanel\nQuiz\nRevisionMode\nFieldGuide\nFooter\n\nUse React Router for navigation.\n\nUse local structured data rather than a backend database.\n\n==================================================\n19. RESPONSIVE DESIGN\n==================================================\n\nThe website must work beautifully on:\n\nDesktop\nLaptop\nTablet\nMobile\n\nOn mobile:\n- cards should stack properly\n- navigation should collapse\n- images should remain readable\n- specimen details should be easy to scroll\n- quiz should work comfortably with touch\n\n==================================================\n20. ANIMATIONS\n==================================================\n\nUse tasteful animations.\n\nExamples:\n- fade-in on scroll\n- smooth section transitions\n- card hover\n- image zoom\n- page transitions\n- subtle floating botanical elements\n- smooth scrolling\n- specimen modal animation\n\nAnimations must be subtle and elegant.\n\nAvoid:\n- excessive bouncing\n- flashy transitions\n- distracting animations\n\nThe website should feel calm and premium.\n\n==================================================\n21. TYPOGRAPHY\n==================================================\n\nUse a combination of:\n\nElegant serif font for major headings.\n\nClean sans-serif font for body text.\n\nThe overall typography should resemble:\n- natural history books\n- scientific journals\n- museum exhibits\n\nMake scientific names italic.\n\n==================================================\n22. BACKGROUND\n==================================================\n\nCreate a layered background system.\n\nBase:\nwarm cream / parchment tone.\n\nSecondary:\nvery subtle paper grain.\n\nDecorative:\nfaint leaves / botanical forms / marine silhouettes.\n\nSome sections can transition into:\nsage green\nforest green\nwarm beige\nsoft terracotta\n\nThe background should visually connect the entire website.\n\n==================================================\n23. FOOTER\n==================================================\n\nCreate a simple elegant footer.\n\nInclude:\n\n\"NON-CHORDATA\"\n\n\"An Interactive Zoology Field Guide\"\n\nSections:\nExplore\nPhyla\nSpecimens\nRevision\nQuiz\n\nAdd:\n\n\"Built as an educational resource for studying Non-Chordata.\"\n\n==================================================\n24. IMPORTANT UX REQUIREMENTS\n==================================================\n\nThe website should never feel overwhelming.\n\nUse progressive disclosure.\n\nShow:\noverview first\n\342\206\222 details when requested\n\342\206\222 deeper information inside specimen pages\n\nAdd breadcrumbs:\n\nHome\n\342\206\222 Phyla\n\342\206\222 Porifera\n\342\206\222 Sycon\n\nAdd a \"Back to Phylum\" button.\n\nAdd \"Previous Specimen\" and \"Next Specimen\" navigation.\n\n==================================================\n25. DATA ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nSince there are only around 25 specimens, DO NOT create a complicated backend.\n\nCreate a structured data file:\n\nsrc/data/nonChordata.ts\n\nExample:\n\nexport const phyla = [...]\n\nexport const specimens = [...]\n\nEach specimen should reference its phylum using an ID.\n\nExample:\n\n{\n  id: \"sycon\",\n  name: \"Sycon\",\n  scientificName: \"Sycon\",\n  phylumId: \"porifera\",\n  description: \"...\",\n  habitat: \"...\",\n  morphology: \"...\",\n  reproduction: \"...\",\n  importance: \"...\",\n  examPoints: [...]\n}\n\nThis should make it extremely easy to add new specimens later.\n\n==================================================\n26. SEARCH ARCHITECTURE\n==================================================\n\nImplement client-side search.\n\nSearch should instantly search through:\n\n- Phylum names\n- Specimen names\n- Scientific names\n- Keywords\n\nShow results in a clean dropdown.\n\nExample:\n\nSearch: \"sponge\"\n\nResults:\nPorifera\nSycon\nHyalonema\nEuplectella\nSpongilla\n\n==================================================\n27. PERFORMANCE\n==================================================\n\nKeep the website lightweight.\n\nDo not add unnecessary libraries.\n\nOptimize images.\n\nLazy-load specimen images where appropriate.\n\nAvoid unnecessary API calls.\n\nNo backend is required for the initial version.\n\n==================================================\n28. ACCESSIBILITY\n==================================================\n\nUse:\n- semantic HTML\n- alt text for images\n- keyboard navigation\n- accessible buttons\n- readable contrast\n- proper heading hierarchy\n\n==================================================\n29. CONTENT QUALITY\n==================================================\n\nThe content is intended for undergraduate zoology study.\n\nMake information:\n- scientifically accurate\n- structured\n- exam-friendly\n- understandable\n- concise but sufficiently detailed\n\nAvoid overly complicated research-level terminology unless it is explained.\n\nFor important terminology, add small tooltips.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Choanocytes\"\n\342\206\222 \"Specialized flagellated cells involved in water current generation and food capture in sponges.\"\n\n==================================================\n30. SPECIAL VISUAL FEATURE\n==================================================\n\nCreate a \"Specimen Spotlight\" component.\n\nWhen the user opens a specimen, show:\n\n----------------------------------\nSPECIMEN SPOTLIGHT\n\n        [IMAGE]\n\n        SYCON\n        Porifera\n\n        \"A simple but fascinating\n         example of a sponge.\"\n\n        Habitat: Marine\n        Organization: Cellular\n\n        [Explore Anatomy]\n----------------------------------\n\nUse a subtle natural-paper card design.\n\n==================================================\n31. ANATOMY INTERACTION\n==================================================\n\nWhere appropriate, create simple interactive anatomy diagrams.\n\nFor example, for Sycon:\n\nOsculum\n\342\206\223\nSpongocoel\n\342\206\223\nRadial canals\n\342\206\223\nIncurrent canals\n\342\206\223\nOstia\n\nClicking a label should show a short explanation.\n\nFor other organisms, only create anatomy diagrams where they are genuinely useful.\n\nDo not fabricate scientifically inaccurate diagrams.\n\n==================================================\n32. FINAL VISUAL DIRECTION\n==================================================\n\nImagine this website as a combination of:\n\nNatural History Museum\n+\nUniversity Zoology Lab\n+\nVintage Field Journal\n+\nModern Interactive Educational Website\n\nIt should feel:\n\nElegant\nNatural\nScientific\nEducational\nInteractive\nCalm\nPremium\nImmersive\n\nNOT:\n\nCorporate\nCyberpunk\nAI-generated-looking\nOverly futuristic\nGeneric\nDashboard-like\n\n==================================================\n33. DEVELOPMENT REQUIREMENT\n==================================================\n\nBuild the website completely.\n\nDo not just create a landing page.\n\nAll major interactions must actually work:\n\n\342\234\223 Phylum navigation\n\342\234\223 Specimen navigation\n\342\234\223 Search\n\342\234\223 Filters\n\342\234\223 Specimen details\n\342\234\223 Classification tree\n\342\234\223 Field Guide mode\n\342\234\223 Quiz\n\342\234\223 Revision mode\n\342\234\223 Compare specimens\n\342\234\223 Responsive navigation\n\342\234\223 Previous/Next specimen\n\342\234\223 Breadcrumb navigation\n\nUse realistic sample content for all listed specimens.\n\nDo not leave empty placeholder sections such as:\n\"Lorem ipsum\"\n\"Coming soon\"\n\"Add content here\"\n\n==================================================\n34. FINAL CHECK\n==================================================\n\nBefore finishing, verify:\n\n1. All 5 phyla exist.\n2. All listed specimens exist.\n3. Every specimen opens its own detail view.\n4. Every specimen has an image field.\n5. Search works.\n6. Filters work.\n7. Quiz works.\n8. Comparison works.\n9. Field Guide works.\n10. Revision mode works.\n11. Navigation works.\n12. Mobile layout works.\n13. No broken routes.\n14. No console errors.\n15. No placeholder content.\n16. The visual design consistently follows the earthy/natural-history theme.\n\nStart by creating the complete frontend architecture and data model, then implement the visual design and interactions.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not over-engineer this project.\nThere are only 5 phyla and approximately 25 specimens.\nPrioritize visual quality, educational usefulness, interaction and a polished user experience over backend complexity."
    }
  }
}
