  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Reviewing Requirements Carefully**\n\nOkay, I\'m now meticulously reviewing the project requirements, specifically focusing on updating the `metadata.json` and `index.html` files. I need to make sure the title, meta tags, and fonts are all correctly implemented. The font choices should incorporate some elegance and readability. I am considering fonts like Cormorant Garamond and Playfair Display alongside Plus Jakarta Sans or Lora.\n\n\n**Refining Font and Data**\n\nI\'ve finalized the font pairing; I\'m importing Cinzel, Cormorant Garamond, and Plus Jakarta Sans. My focus now is the phyla and specimen data. I have determined the exact 5 phyla, and started drafting out the specimen list for both *Porifera* and *Cnidaria* phyla. I\'m aiming to create a comprehensive and accurate list.\n\n\n**Confirming Specimen Data**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the specimen list and it looks like 22 primary specimens, with potential to add up to three more as needed to align with the requested range of 22-25. The Phyla and specimens are finalized. I\'m also now shifting focus to the image mapping requirement. My plan is to create a central data file, specifically `src/data/images.ts`, to manage image URLs, ensuring no hardcoding occurs. This will export the image map. We can leverage the generated plate images from `/src/assets/images/...` as a starting point.\n\n\n**Defining Image Resources**\n\nI am now focusing on the image data structure. I\'m building out the `src/data/images.ts` file, and defining the mapping for each specimen, including `primary`, `secondary`, `plate`, `thumbnail`, `caption`, and `credit` URLs for high-quality Wikimedia Commons images, naturalist illustrations, or fallbacks. I\'m also considering the integration of SVG anatomical line art and graceful fallback options to meet the image requirements.\n\n**Constructing Hero and Navigation**\n\nNow I\'m designing the hero section and navigation. I\'ll set the title as \"NON-CHORDATA\" and subtitle as \"An Interactive Journey Through the Hidden World of Invertebrate Life,\" along with supporting text. A prominent \"Explore the Phyla\" CTA will smooth scroll. I\'ll use a beautiful natural lithograph background with an earthy palette. The navigation will be a sticky navbar with a clean three-zone contract, with links for Home, Phyla, Specimens, Explore, Tree, Field Guide, Revision, Quiz, and About and include a search icon/bar with instant client-side autocomplete.\n\n\n**Designing Mobile Layouts**\n\nI\'m now implementing a mobile-responsive menu. This includes designing five interactive cards, each representing a phylum (Porifera, Cnidaria, Ctenophora, Platyhelminthes, Nemathelminthes), for the \"Phyla Exploration\" section. Each card will showcase the phylum\'s name, a brief description, specimen count, and a representative visual, with an \"Explore Phylum\" button. I\'m aiming for a natural museum specimen card styling. I\'m also planning the \"Phylum Detail View,\" which will provide an overview, general characteristics, and a grid of specimens.\n\n\n**Developing Specimen Views**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the individual specimen experience, implementing the `/specimens/:specimenId` route. This will deliver a full-screen naturalist dossier. I\'m prioritizing the inclusion of a high-quality image, complete with a zoom modal and a descriptive caption. I\'m also preparing to integrate a scientific classification table, identification details, habitat information, external and internal morphology descriptions, including interactive glossary tooltips, mode of nutrition, and information on reproduction, ecological importance, interesting facts, and exam points. Navigation will be available via breadcrumbs. I\'ll also add buttons to navigate to the phylum, previous, and next specimen. Finally, I\'m integrating a \"Specimen Spotlight\" component for extra engagement.\n\n\n**Implementing Interactive Features**\n\nI\'m now building out key interactive features. I\'m excited about the side-by-side specimen comparison tool and the interactive classification tree that jumps between taxonomic definitions and specific specimens. I am also working on the \"Field Guide\" mode, and drafting a \"Test Your Zoology\" quiz for the specimen data. I\'m focusing on user engagement.\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_1486398"
      name: "edit_file"
      arguments_json: "{\"Instruction\":\"Update metadata.json with the name and description of the Non-Chordata Zoology Field Guide\", \"ReplacementContent\":\"{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"Non-Chordata Natural History Field Guide\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"An interactive digital zoology atlas and natural history field guide covering five major Non-Chordate phyla and representative specimens for undergraduate zoology.\\\",\\n  \\\"requestFramePermissions\\\": [],\\n  \\\"majorCapabilities\\\": [\\\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\\\"]\\n}\", \"TargetContent\":\"{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"description\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"requestFramePermissions\\\": [],\\n  \\\"majorCapabilities\\\": [\\\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\\\"]\\n}\", \"TargetFile\":\"/metadata.json\", \"toolAction\":\"Updating metadata.json\", \"toolSummary\":\"Update app metadata name and description\"}"
