  planner_response {
    response: "The **Non-Chordata Natural History Field Guide & Zoology Atlas** is built, adhering to an archival museum and naturalist field journal aesthetic.\n\n### What Was Built\n\n1. **Natural History & Field Journal Visual Design**:\n   - Curated botanical and naturalist palette: warm parchment, moss green, deep forest, terracotta, antique ink, and limestone.\n   - Antique typography pairing: Cormorant Garamond / Cinzel editorial headings, Lora book serif, and JetBrains Mono for accession tags.\n   - Archival lithographs, museum specimen labels, accession stamps, and delicate paper textures without futuristic gradients or SaaS cards.\n\n2. **The 5 Phyla & 22+ Laboratory Specimens**:\n   - **Porifera**: *Sycon*, *Hyalonema*, *Euplectella*, *Spongilla*.\n   - **Cnidaria**: *Obelia*, *Physalia*, *Millepora*, *Aurelia*, *Tubipora*, *Corallium*, *Alcyonium*, *Gorgonia*, *Adamsia*, *Pennatula*, *Fungia*, *Meandrina*, *Madrepora*.\n   - **Ctenophora**: *Beroe*, *Ctenoplana*.\n   - **Platyhelminthes**: *Fasciola* (Liver fluke), *Taenia* (Pork tapeworm).\n   - **Nemathelminthes**: *Ascaris* (Giant intestinal roundworm).\n\n3. **Phylum Detail Views**:\n   - Comprehensive coverage of all 15 zoological sections: Overview, Habitat, Symmetry, Level of Organization, Germ Layers, Coelom, Body Wall, Canal/Coelenteron, Digestion, Respiration, Excretion, Nervous Coordination, Reproduction & Larvae, Skeleton, and Ecological/Economic Importance.\n   - \"At a Glance\" definition summary cards.\n   - Interactive anatomical pathway diagrams (e.g., Syconoid canal system flow, Cnidocyte firing kinetics, Ctenophore aboral statocyst coordination, Flame cell ultrastructure, and Ascaris body mechanics).\n\n4. **Individual Specimen Dossiers**:\n   - Museum-grade specimen photographs and scientific plates with zoom capability and resilient zero-broken-image fallbacks.\n   - Complete Linnaean Classification tables (Kingdom down to species).\n   - \"Specimen Spotlight\" natural-paper card.\n   - Interactive anatomical flowcharts (such as *Fasciola* digenetic larval succession, *Taenia* proglottid strobilation, and *Ascaris* pulmonary migration).\n   - Undergraduate exam revision points and curator\'s trivia notes.\n   - Breadcrumb navigation and Previous/Next specimen controls.\n\n5. **Interactive Exploration & Learning Modes**:\n   - **Specimen Explorer & Search**: Instant client-side search across common names, scientific binomials, phyla, and anatomical terms, coupled with a phylum filter.\n   - **Comparative Anatomy Tool**: Side-by-side comparative analysis of any two selected specimens across symmetry, organization, nutrition, reproduction, and adaptations.\n   - **Interactive Classification Tree**: Expandable cladogram tracing Kingdom Animalia down to Parazoa, Radiata, Bilateria, Acoelomata, Pseudocoelomata, phyla, and species nodes.\n   - **Naturalist\'s Field Guide Mode**: Interactive journal folio allowing students to flip through illustrated plates with handwritten-style field notes and accession stamps.\n   - **Test Your Zoology Quiz**: Multiple-choice, spotter, and true/false questions with immediate pedagogical feedback and score tracking.\n   - **Exam Revision & Practical Spotters**: Systematic comparison matrix and diagnostic keys for microscope slides and lab jars.\n   - **Centralized Image Mapping**: Located in `src/data/images.ts` for clean future asset customization without touching UI logic."
    modified_response: "The **Non-Chordata Natural History Field Guide & Zoology Atlas** is built, adhering to an archival museum and naturalist field journal aesthetic.\n\n### What Was Built\n\n1. **Natural History & Field Journal Visual Design**:\n   - Curated botanical and naturalist palette: warm parchment, moss green, deep forest, terracotta, antique ink, and limestone.\n   - Antique typography pairing: Cormorant Garamond / Cinzel editorial headings, Lora book serif, and JetBrains Mono for accession tags.\n   - Archival lithographs, museum specimen labels, accession stamps, and delicate paper textures without futuristic gradients or SaaS cards.\n\n2. **The 5 Phyla & 22+ Laboratory Specimens**:\n   - **Porifera**: *Sycon*, *Hyalonema*, *Euplectella*, *Spongilla*.\n   - **Cnidaria**: *Obelia*, *Physalia*, *Millepora*, *Aurelia*, *Tubipora*, *Corallium*, *Alcyonium*, *Gorgonia*, *Adamsia*, *Pennatula*, *Fungia*, *Meandrina*, *Madrepora*.\n   - **Ctenophora**: *Beroe*, *Ctenoplana*.\n   - **Platyhelminthes**: *Fasciola* (Liver fluke), *Taenia* (Pork tapeworm).\n   - **Nemathelminthes**: *Ascaris* (Giant intestinal roundworm).\n\n3. **Phylum Detail Views**:\n   - Comprehensive coverage of all 15 zoological sections: Overview, Habitat, Symmetry, Level of Organization, Germ Layers, Coelom, Body Wall, Canal/Coelenteron, Digestion, Respiration, Excretion, Nervous Coordination, Reproduction & Larvae, Skeleton, and Ecological/Economic Importance.\n   - \"At a Glance\" definition summary cards.\n   - Interactive anatomical pathway diagrams (e.g., Syconoid canal system flow, Cnidocyte firing kinetics, Ctenophore aboral statocyst coordination, Flame cell ultrastructure, and Ascaris body mechanics).\n\n4. **Individual Specimen Dossiers**:\n   - Museum-grade specimen photographs and scientific plates with zoom capability and resilient zero-broken-image fallbacks.\n   - Complete Linnaean Classification tables (Kingdom down to species).\n   - \"Specimen Spotlight\" natural-paper card.\n   - Interactive anatomical flowcharts (such as *Fasciola* digenetic larval succession, *Taenia* proglottid strobilation, and *Ascaris* pulmonary migration).\n   - Undergraduate exam revision points and curator\'s trivia notes.\n   - Breadcrumb navigation and Previous/Next specimen controls.\n\n5. **Interactive Exploration & Learning Modes**:\n   - **Specimen Explorer & Search**: Instant client-side search across common names, scientific binomials, phyla, and anatomical terms, coupled with a phylum filter.\n   - **Comparative Anatomy Tool**: Side-by-side comparative analysis of any two selected specimens across symmetry, organization, nutrition, reproduction, and adaptations.\n   - **Interactive Classification Tree**: Expandable cladogram tracing Kingdom Animalia down to Parazoa, Radiata, Bilateria, Acoelomata, Pseudocoelomata, phyla, and species nodes.\n   - **Naturalist\'s Field Guide Mode**: Interactive journal folio allowing students to flip through illustrated plates with handwritten-style field notes and accession stamps.\n   - **Test Your Zoology Quiz**: Multiple-choice, spotter, and true/false questions with immediate pedagogical feedback and score tracking.\n   - **Exam Revision & Practical Spotters**: Systematic comparison matrix and diagnostic keys for microscope slides and lab jars.\n   - **Centralized Image Mapping**: Located in `src/data/images.ts` for clean future asset customization without touching UI logic."
